verdicts.evidence:
ONE PLACE EXISTS. components/data/format.js holds the pack shape and the shipped default: "export const IN_PACK = { id: "IN", locale: "en-IN", currency: "INR", currencySymbol: "₹", ... clock: "24h", taxIdLabel: "GSTIN", ... }", "export const GENERIC_PACK = {...}", "export function createFormat(pack = {}) { const p = { ...IN_PACK, ...pack };" returning "{ pack: p, number, money, compact, compactMoney, time, date, parseNumber }", and "export const IN_FORMAT = createFormat(IN_PACK);". components/data/MarketProvider.jsx wires it: "const FormatContext = React.createContext(IN_FORMAT);" / "export function useFormat() { return React.useContext(FormatContext); }".

EVERY FILE THE GAP NAMED, CHECKED INDIVIDUALLY:
• components/charts/Charts.jsx — all four chart types now take their format: BarChart, LineChart, DonutChart and FunnelChart each contain "const mkt = useFormat();
  const fmt = format || mkt.number;". `num` is gone; `inr` survives only as an explicit IN shortcut, no longer a default: "/* Formats come from the market pack (F1 / F3-20), not from this file. `inr` remains as the IN
   pack's money shortcut for callers that genuinely mean rupees; everything else asks the provider... */
export const inr = (n) => IN_FORMAT.money(n);".
• components/feedback/UsageMeter.jsx — the baked const is gone: "const mkt = useFormat();
  const fmt = mkt.number;", used for value, limit, overage and aria-valuetext.
• components/brand/DocumentPreview.jsx — "const mkt = useFormat();
  const amount = (v) => (typeof v === "number" ? mkt.money(v) : v);
  const taxLabel = taxIdLabel || mkt.pack.taxIdLabel;" with the comment "Amounts are NUMBERS: the document renders them in the tenant's market format (F1 / F3-20) rather than trusting each caller to pre-format Indian grouping." The `gstin` prop is gone — DocumentPreview.d.ts now has "taxId?: string;" and "/** What that number is called — defaults to the market pack ("GSTIN" here, "VAT number" elsewhere). */
  taxIdLabel?: string;" and "lineItems?: [string, number | string][];".
• components/forms/TimeField.jsx — "const mkt = useFormat();
  /* What the user reads and types. The prop, the commit and the bounds stay 24-hour. */
  const show = (v) => (v ? mkt.time(v) : "");
  const ph = placeholder || (mkt.pack.clock === "12h" ? "h:mm AM" : "HH:MM");" with a 12-hour parser "const RE12 = /^(1[0-2]|0?[1-9])[:.\s]?([0-5]\d)\s*([ap])\.?m?\.?$/i;". TimeField.d.ts no longer asserts a 24-hour display: "**The value is always 24-hour**, in every market... What the user reads and types follows the active market pack's clock, and both spellings parse on entry."
• components/forms/NumberField — ENTRY is covered, not just display. NumberField.d.ts: "/**
   * Display the amount in the active market's currency and grouping (F1 / F3-20). The field is
   * EDITED as a plain number and the committed value is always a number — formatting never leaks
   * into it.
   */
  currency?: boolean;" and NumberField.jsx: "const display = (v) => (currency ? mkt.money(v) : String(v));" / "const parsed = currency ? mkt.parseNumber(draft) : parseFloat(draft);".

verdicts.stillMissing:
The DATE leg of the gap statement ("Money, dates, times and large numbers rendered AND ENTERED in the active market pack's formats"). The pack declares a date formatter — components/data/MarketProvider.d.ts: "date: (value: string | Date) => string;" implemented in format.js as "return new Intl.DateTimeFormat(p.locale, { day: "numeric", month: "short", year: "numeric" }).format(d);" — but no component consumes it. The one component where dates are both rendered and entered ignores the pack entirely. components/forms/DatePicker.jsx:
  "const MONTHS = ["January", "February", "March", ...];
const DAYS = ["Mon", "Tue", "Wed", "Thu", "Fri", "Sat", "Sun"];"
  "/** 12 Mar 2026 — the product's date format. */
export function formatDate(v) {
  const d = parse(v);
  if (!d) return "";
  return `${d.getDate()} ${MONTHS[d.getMonth()].slice(0, 3)} ${d.getFullYear()}`;
}"
There is no `useFormat` import in the file; English month names, day-month-year order and a Monday week start ("const offset = (first.getDay() + 6) % 7;") are all hardcoded, and components/forms/DatePicker.d.ts still calls it "/** Formats a date or ISO string as "12 Mar 2026". */" with no pack mention. DocumentPreview likewise passes its date straight through rather than through `mkt.date`: "docDate = "16 Aug 2026"," rendered as "{docDate}". So a market can replace currency, grouping, clock, compact form and tax-ID label, but not the date format.

verdicts.evidence:
WHERE IT LIVES. tokens/field-mode.css exists and is a second token scope, imported last so it wins at equal specificity over the :root defaults. styles.css:
  "@import "tokens/colors.css";" ... "@import "tokens/base.css";
@import "tokens/field-mode.css";"
tokens/field-mode.css opens the scope on the attribute, not :root:
  "[data-field-mode="on"]{
  /* Surfaces separate harder... */
  --canvas:#FFFFFF;
  --canvas-sunken:#E9ECF0;" ... "--text-primary:#000000;   /* 21.00 */
  --text-secondary:#2B2D31; /* 13.86 */
  --text-tertiary:#35383D;  /* 11.73 */" ... "--success-text:#07542C;
  --warning-text:#5C3A05;
  --danger-text:#8E1519;
  --info-text:#0F4199;
  --neutral-text:#3A3D42;"

WHAT THE SWITCH IS. components/forms/FieldModeToggle.jsx:
  "const ATTR = "data-field-mode";
const KEY = "hg-field-mode";"
  "function apply(on) {
  if (typeof document === "undefined") return;
  document.documentElement.setAttribute(ATTR, on ? "on" : "off");
  try { window.localStorage.setItem(KEY, on ? "on" : "off"); } catch { /* private mode */ }
}"
  "export function useFieldMode() {
  const [on, setOn] = React.useState(read);
  React.useEffect(() => { apply(on); }, [on]);
  return [on, setOn];
}"
  "export function FieldModeToggle({ label = "High-contrast field mode", hint = "Stronger text, visible edges on every card and control. For working in direct sunlight.", ... }) {" — rendering a real `<Switch checked={on} onChange={(e) => setOn(e.target.checked)} .../>`. Also "FieldModeToggle.set = apply;" for a settings screen or QA harness.

HOW IT REACHES INLINE-STYLED COMPONENTS WITH NO PROP — this is the load-bearing part and it is real. tokens/field-mode.css:
  "HOW IT REACHES INLINE-STYLED COMPONENTS WITHOUT !important.
   Every component in this system reads its colours and its elevation from custom properties. So
   the mode overrides the PROPERTIES, and the components inherit the change with no per-component
   prop, no field-mode branch and no specificity fight"
  "--field-edge:#5B6069;

  /* THE BORDER LAW YIELDING, in five lines: every elevation gains a hairline ring... */
  --e1:0 0 0 1px var(--field-edge), 0 1px 2px rgba(16,24,40,0.10);
  --e2:0 0 0 1px var(--field-edge), 0 2px 6px rgba(16,24,40,0.10);
  --e3:0 0 0 1px var(--field-edge), 0 6px 16px rgba(16,24,40,0.12);
  --e4:0 0 0 1px var(--field-edge), 0 10px 28px rgba(16,24,40,0.14);
  --e5:0 0 0 1.5px var(--field-edge), 0 18px 44px rgba(16,24,40,0.18);"
Those override tokens/elevation.css's ":root{ ... --e1:0 1px 2px rgba(16,24,40,0.04);
  --e2:0 2px 8px rgba(16,24,40,0.05); ... }" — equal specificity, later import wins, and both selectors match the same <html> element the toggle stamps.
The inheritance claim checks out against the actual inline styles: components/data/StatCard.jsx "boxShadow: "var(--e2)"", components/data/ListRow.jsx "boxShadow: hover ? "var(--e2)" : "none"", components/forms/NumberField.jsx "boxShadow: focus ? "0 0 0 2px var(--accent)" : "var(--e1)"" and "color: "var(--text-primary)"" — every one is a token read, so the attribute alone changes them.
Gap-filling rule for controls with no inline shadow, plus focus:
  "[data-field-mode="on"] :is(button,[role="tab"],[role="button"]):not(:disabled){
  box-shadow:inset 0 0 0 1.5px var(--field-edge);
}
/* Focus stays the loudest thing on screen. */
[data-field-mode="on"] :focus-visible{ outline:3px solid var(--accent); outline-offset:2px; }"

NO PER-COMPONENT PROP, stated in the type file — components/forms/FieldModeToggle.d.ts: "It is global (one attribute on `<html>`), persisted, and reaches every component through the token layer — no component takes a field-mode prop." I found no `fieldMode` prop in any component read.

THE TWO FALSE ASSERTIONS ARE NOW TRUE. readme.md: "Field mode is real, not an aspiration: `tokens/field-mode.css` scopes `:root[data-field-mode="on"]` and `FieldModeToggle` switches it." components/forms/Dropzone.d.ts's claim now has an implementation behind it: "the 1.5px dashed edge is one of only two legal exceptions to the system's no-borders rule (the other is the opt-in high-contrast field mode)". A specimen proves both modes render side by side — guidelines/field-mode.card.html: "<div className="stage" data-field-mode={mode}>" with "Nothing on the right takes a field-mode prop. The mode overrides the <b>tokens</b>".

(One deviation from the readme's wording, not from the capability: the CSS selector is the bare attribute "[data-field-mode="on"]", not ":root[data-field-mode="on"]" as readme.md says — deliberate, per the file's own comment "The selector is the ATTRIBUTE, not :root — the product sets it on <html>, and a specimen or a preview pane can set it on a subtree to show both modes at once." That is what makes the side-by-side card work.)

A top bar now exists on BOTH platforms as real system components.

