    rail={ <AppRail value={view} onChange={setView} ... avatar={<Avatar name="Amit Rane" size={36} />} /> }
    header={
      <AppHeader title="Leads" subtitle={`${rows.length} leads · ...`}
        search={<SearchField density="functional" placeholder="Search leads, projects, invoices" value={gq} onChange={setGq} onClear={() => setGq("")} />}
        notifications={7} onNotificationsClick={() => {}}
        actions={<Button size="sm" ...>Add lead</Button>}
        avatar={<Avatar name="Amit Rane" size={32} />} />
    }>
and (mobile):
  <MobileTopBar brand={<LogoTile size={28} radius={9} />} title="Leads" onSearchClick={() => {}}
    notifications={7} onNotificationsClick={() => {}}
    avatar={<Avatar name="Amit Rane" size={32} />} />

The count is carried by a new `CountBadge` in components/navigation/AppShell.jsx, and the number is literal on-screen text plus accessible text.

  export function CountBadge({ count, max = 99, label = "items", tone = "danger", style = {} }) {
    const n = typeof count === "number" ? count : null;
    if (n !== null && n <= 0) return null;
    if (n === null && !count) return null;
    const shown = n === null ? null : n > max ? `${max}+` : String(n);
    const words = n === null ? `Unread ${label}` : `${n} unread ${label}`;
    return (
      <span style={{ ... fontVariantNumeric: "tabular-nums", ... }}>
        {shown}
        <span style={{ position: "absolute", width: 1, height: 1, overflow: "hidden", clip: "rect(0 0 0 0)" }}>{words}</span>
      </span>
    );
  }

The boolean is gone from the rail item type. components/navigation/AppRail.d.ts:
  /**
   * Unread count, read **from the record** (`F6-17`) so it always matches the list it opens.
   * Renders the numeral via `CountBadge` — "99+" above 99, nothing at zero. `true` still draws a
   * bare dot for the rare case where a count isn't known; prefer a number.
   */
  badge?: number | boolean;

The number reaches the accessible name on every button that can carry it. components/navigation/AppRail.jsx (RailButton):
  aria-label={item.badge != null && item.badge !== false ? `${item.label}, ${typeof item.badge === "number" ? item.badge + " unread" : "unread"}` : item.label}
and the badge itself:
  {item.badge != null && item.badge !== false && (
    <span style={{ position: "absolute", top: 4, right: 2 }}><CountBadge count={item.badge} label={item.label.toLowerCase()} /></span>
  )}
components/navigation/AppShell.jsx (ShellAction, the bell in both top bars):
  <button type="button" aria-label={badge ? `${label}, ${typeof badge === "number" ? badge : "unread"}` : label}
BottomNav's NavItem renders the same CountBadge, so the count exists on the phone shell too.

AppShell.d.ts states the rule: "The unread count. A **numeral**, not a red dot: a dot is the mark with the word removed — it can't match the list it stands for ... and fails `F7-12` on the surface every other screen copies. Renders nothing at zero."

Two notes that do not change the verdict (the capability and the words are present): (a) ShellAction's numeric name reads "Notifications, 7" while RailButton's reads "Leads, 7 unread" — the shell button drops the word "unread"; (b) templates/desktop-web-app/app.jsx and templates/mobile-field-app/app.jsx still pass the documented dot fallback — `footer={[{ key: "alerts", label: "Notifications", icon: <IcBell />, badge: true }, ...]}` — so those two templates show a bare dot rather than a number. That is a caller choice against a fallback the API deliberately keeps, not a missing capability.

verdicts.stillMissing:
Two residues, neither of them a mis-measured target.

1. Menu.d.ts STILL DOCUMENTS 32px — the exact line the previous verification flagged, unchanged. components/overlays/Menu.d.ts:
   /** Custom trigger element. Defaults to a 32px overflow button. */
   trigger?: React.ReactElement;
The implementation is 44×44; the published type file tells a caller it is 32. Nothing else in Menu.d.ts corrects it, and it is the only one of the four sites whose types were not updated (compare AppShell.d.ts, which says "A 44×44 shell button").

2. A fifth tappable control at 32px, outside the written exception, was not covered by the fix. components/feedback/Banner.jsx:
   /** Inline text action for a banner — "Take the new value", "Pay now". Never a filled button. */
   export function BannerAction({ children, onClick, style = {} }) {
     return (
       <button type="button" onClick={onClick}
         style={{ minHeight: 32, padding: "0 12px", border: "none", borderRadius: "var(--r-pill)", ... }}>
That is a 32px-tall hit area with no expressed 44px floor — no wrapper, no negative margin, nothing like FilterBar's "target and the visible pill are two different rectangles" treatment. It is not inside a data row and it is not pointer-only (banners are a mobile surface), so readme.md's one narrow exception — "a pointer-only affordance *inside* a data row (the call / message buttons in a leads table)" — does not reach it. The genuinely exempted 32px controls, the templates' `RowActions` call/message buttons (`width: 32, height: 32` in templates/*/app.jsx), do qualify and are correctly left alone.
